#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370953 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67a0d7e9-a72d-5fc5-b7f4-cd87aaa4ba3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-42923" :question/problem "Find the determinant of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle F=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $3$rd column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(F) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 0 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & -1 & {\\color{lightgray}{-2}} \\\\ 4 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & =-1\\cdot (3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-3))=-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet(F)=-9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371063 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d109fab1-7a0a-532d-8a65-68e9e49778da" :question/math-academy-id "q-75067" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},$ ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371070 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "82af09e9-f65e-5eb5-8267-28d6aa4538d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-76338" :question/problem "The vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1},$ ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2},$ ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{4}$ are given below. Determine whether $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3},{\\mathbf{a}}_{4}\\}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3},$ where ${\\mathbf{a}}_{4}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3},{\\mathbf{a}}_{4}\\}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}{x}_{1}}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}}+{\\color{rblack}{x}_{2}}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}}+{\\color{rblack}{x}_{3}}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=-4 \\\\ -9{x}_{1}-8{x}_{3} &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & -4 \\\\ -9 & 0 & -8 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0 & -8 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+9{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 17\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 0 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 17\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3},{\\mathbf{a}}_{4}\\}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=-15,$ ${x}_{2}=0,$ and ${x}_{3}=17,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n(-15)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}}+0\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}}+17\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=(-15)+0+17=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371077 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "92efbe12-f093-58f1-a6e2-e6fbb71066d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-77486" :question/problem "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $4$th row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using cofactor expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ {\\color{red}3} & {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}1}\\end{vmatrix} & ={\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{C}_{41}}+{\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{42}}+{\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{43}}+{\\color{red}1}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{44}} \\\\  & ={\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{blue}(-1)^{4+1}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}}+{\\color{red}1}\\cdot {\\color{blue}(-1)^{4+4}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\  & =-3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNow, computing the $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain: \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ {\\color{red}2} & {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} \\\\ 1 & -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & ={\\color{red}2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{C}_{21}}+{\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{22}}+{\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{23}} \\\\  & ={\\color{red}2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}(-1)^{2+1}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\  & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2(16-(-2)) \\\\  & =-36\\end{aligned}$$\n\n \nFinally, we compute the second $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along its $3$rd row:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}1} & {\\color{red}-2}\\end{vmatrix} & ={\\color{red}0}\\cdot {\\color{black}{C}_{31}}+{\\color{red}1}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{C}_{32}}+{\\color{red}(-2)}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{C}_{33}} \\\\  & ={\\color{red}1}\\cdot {\\color{blue}(-1)^{3+2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}}+{\\color{red}(-2)}\\cdot {\\color{blue}(-1)^{3+3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(0-4)-2(2-2) \\\\  & =4+0 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=-3\\cdot (-36)+1\\cdot 4=112.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371119 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f896c29-487a-51e0-95fe-118554647621" :question/math-academy-id "q-81116" :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $\\mathbf{x}=[{x}_{1},\\hspace{0.167em}{x}_{2}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|?$\n\n$$\nU=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vector of $U$ as ${\\mathbf{u}}_{1}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$, we need to find a second vector $\\mathbf{x}$ such that $‖\\mathbf{x}‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot \\mathbf{x}=0\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{1}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{2}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{x}_{2}\\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we obtain the vector \n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $\\mathbf{x}.$ Notice that $‖\\mathbf{x}‖=\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}=5.$ So, we normalize $\\mathbf{x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathbf{x}}{‖\\mathbf{x}‖}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle |{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|=|\\frac{4}{5}|+|-\\frac{3}{5}|=\\frac{7}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371133 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2ecf41d-bb8c-5910-8164-c718a9bc187a" :question/math-academy-id "q-82400" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{\\mathbf{u}}_{1},{\\mathbf{u}}_{2}\\},$\n$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}\\},$ and \n$\\{{\\mathbf{w}}_{1},{\\mathbf{w}}_{2}\\}$, respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $$\n{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{2}=0\\hspace{1em}\\text{and}\\hspace{1em}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {\\mathbf{u}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- $$\n{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2}=0\\hspace{1em}\\text{and}\\hspace{1em}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {\\mathbf{v}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- $$\n{\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{2}=0\\hspace{1em}\\text{and}\\hspace{1em}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {\\mathbf{w}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}=0 & {\\color{green}✓} \\\\ {\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}=1 & {\\color{green}✓} \\\\ {\\mathbf{u}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}=1 & {\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=0+1=1\\ne 0 & {\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\ne 0 & {\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371167 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d55bdef-1f91-5e0a-9c79-2e4df04e0183" :question/math-academy-id "q-83739" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{\\mathbf{u}}_{1},{\\mathbf{u}}_{2}\\},$\n$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}\\},$ and \n$\\{{\\mathbf{w}}_{1},{\\mathbf{w}}_{2}\\}$, respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $$\n{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{2}=0\\hspace{1em}\\text{and}\\hspace{1em}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {\\mathbf{u}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- $$\n{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2}=0\\hspace{1em}\\text{and}\\hspace{1em}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {\\mathbf{v}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- $$\n{\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{2}=0\\hspace{1em}\\text{and}\\hspace{1em}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {\\mathbf{w}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{3}{10}-\\frac{3}{10}=0 & {\\color{green}✓} \\\\ {\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{9}{10}+\\frac{1}{10}=1 & {\\color{green}✓} \\\\ {\\mathbf{u}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{9}{10}=1 & {\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=-1-1=-2\\ne 0 & {\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}=-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}\\ne 0 & {\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371499 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1eebc51b-6af1-57eb-9255-9e266158abc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-105171" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{b},$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\mathbf{b}$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1} \\\\ -{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5{x}_{2} \\\\ -8{x}_{2} \\\\ 5{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=9 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -5 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -8 & 2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -5 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -8 & 2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -5 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -5 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=7 \\\\ 0 &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0=2$  gives a false statement. So the system has no solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373540 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3e3aa161-9904-5811-8bd5-9983d559a3c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-107047" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\in V.$ Given that $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement “$\\{{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{3},{v}_{1}-{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent” is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot ({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})+1\\cdot ({v}_{2}-{v}_{3})+(-1)\\cdot ({v}_{1}-{v}_{3})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})+{x}_{2}({v}_{2}-{v}_{3})+{x}_{3}({v}_{1}-{v}_{3})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that $\\{{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{3},{v}_{1}-{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement “$\\{{v}_{1},2{v}_{2},3{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent” is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}(2{v}_{2})+{x}_{3}(3{v}_{3}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},2{v}_{2},3{v}_{3}\\}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement “$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},0\\}$ is linearly independent” is false. Indeed, notice that \n\n$$\n0\\cdot {v}_{1}+0\\cdot {v}_{2}+1\\cdot 0=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}0=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},0\\}$ is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$\\{{v}_{1},2{v}_{2},3{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374394 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d62b961-39d3-5633-b650-62ddc2233d7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-181159" :question/problem "![](images/99/993cfa437e54f390237b85f3d7ee8a76b626d65431908fe039e0046ba0a33bb1.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,3\\,{{field-1}}\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,6,\\,1\\,{{field-3}}\\,y\\,{{field-4}}\\,4\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the rectangle if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3<x<6,\\hspace{2em}1<y<4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we also need to\n\n- *include* the left edge ($x=3$), and\n\n- *include* the bottom edge ($y=1$).\n\nTherefore, we change the corresponding inequality symbols to non-strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}x<6,\\hspace{2em}1\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}y<4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}3\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}x\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}6,\\hspace{0.222em}1\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}4\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374407 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "adb62f2e-918d-5d56-973b-da3bc299a8de" :question/math-academy-id "q-181602" :question/problem "![](images/79/79df94546a6fafcd66f8733622e8e9d8c37d29c24cb99378eba141610a8fdc8a.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,x-1\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in \\mathbb{Z},y\\in \\mathbb{Z},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{Z}\\times \\mathbb{Z}={\\mathbb{Z}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line $y=x-1.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<x-1\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that some points in our set lie *on* the line. We include these points by changing the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}x-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{Z}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}x-1\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374421 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47c92d4d-2e85-56bd-8425-12a1633613d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-181789" :question/problem "![](images/54/54153dcd97ac6471956b6db34f175d1ee622c9bf74e85f93d5e303a7842d24ed.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,{{field-2}}<x<{{field-3}},\\,{{field-4}}<y<{{field-5}}\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A point $P(x,y)$ lies inside the rectangle if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2<x<6,\\hspace{1em}2<y<5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}2}}<x<\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}6}},\\hspace{0.167em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}2}}<y<\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}5}}\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374453 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c5b2d74-698c-58cf-808c-b4cad536b3ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-181805" :question/problem "![](images/40/40f8e6728cd7108f5026bd87debfbf60b616965686065c8e60f0d3ab741e52e4.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,{{field-2}}\\le x\\le {{field-3}},\\,{{field-4}}\\le y\\le {{field-5}}\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A point $P(x,y)$ lies inside or on the rectangle's edges if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\le x\\le 6,\\hspace{2em}3\\le y\\le 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}}\\le x\\le \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}6}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}3}}\\le y\\le \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}5}}\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374485 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1352187-54c7-5a62-9d05-9f2859a738dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-181811" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d13bfa3f5b0a9456c3251e9363c132578ba0d93b1b5a76c5b375c0c1abc7e310.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em\\}$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}+\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$64$\n\n$\\}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=4$ and vertical radius $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=64.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the ellipse, say, $(5,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n4(5)^{2}+16(0)^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}>}\\hspace{0.222em}64.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}>64.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=64$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\ge }}64.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}4}}{x}^{2}+\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}16}}{y}^{2}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\ge }}64\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375916 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b7ea406-9f82-5933-b31e-095d9f01e027" :question/math-academy-id "q-130646" :question/problem "If $C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${C}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${C}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{2} & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${C}^{2}=C,$ so $C$ is an idempotent matrix.  \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${C}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{4} & ={C}^{2}\\cdot {C}^{2} \\\\  & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & ={C}^{2} \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{C}^{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375944 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8048be79-9af8-51c0-a0c1-5b68f549139c" :question/math-academy-id "q-139986" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\text{Span}\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}?\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is }\\text{invertible}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Col}(A) &=\\mathbb{R}^{4} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(A)) &=4=\\operatorname{rank}(A) \\\\ \\operatorname{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Null}(A)) &=0=\\operatorname{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “The number of non-pivot columns in $A$ is equal to $3$” is false. Since the generating vectors in \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\text{Span}\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\n$$\n\nare linearly independent, we conclude that the number of non-pivot columns in $A$ is equal to $1.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4}$ is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(A)=1\\ne 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(A)=3$ is true. Indeed, for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=4.$ Now, according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+1 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{rank}(A)=3$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375972 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "017db163-2592-561e-a975-d762dc265a45" :question/math-academy-id "q-142542" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\text{Span}\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}?\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is }\\text{invertible}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Col}(A) &=\\mathbb{R}^{5} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(A)) &=5=\\operatorname{rank}(A) \\\\ \\operatorname{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Null}(A)) &=0=\\operatorname{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(A)=3$ is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\text{Span}\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\n$$\n\n \nwe have that $\\text{nullity}(A)=2.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=5,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =5 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =5-2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5}$ is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(A)=2\\ne 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{5}.$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is  invertible” is false. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=3\\ne 5$, according to the  invertible matrix theorem $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{rank}(A)=3$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376047 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77f2cd70-5c51-5abc-8136-b13970866790" :question/math-academy-id "q-156681" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=x+y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ x+y\\end{bmatrix},\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$Π$ contains the zero-vector” is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=x+y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+y \\\\ (0) & =(0)+(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})+({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x+y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x+y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is true. Since statements “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” and “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are true, the plane $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376054 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186669332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55724e97-d014-5bc4-825e-d54d7bbd24c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-157462" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,-1,2)$ and $(1,2,0).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$L$ contains the zero-vector” is false.The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{y+1}{3}=\\frac{z-2}{-2}.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n(0)\\ne \\frac{(0)+1}{3}\\ne \\frac{(0)-2}{-2}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under vector addition” is false. Let's consider the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the point $(1,1,2)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n(1)\\ne \\frac{(1)+1}{3}\\ne \\frac{(2)-2}{-2}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement “$L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is false. Since statements “$L$ is closed under vector addition” and “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376061 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186669333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f7a73387-34bf-50c1-bd13-d6c9025be3b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-157470" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $2x-y+z=2$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ 2-2x+y\\end{bmatrix},\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$Π$ contains the zero-vector” is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y+z & =2 \\\\ 2(0)-(0)+(0) & =2 \\\\ 0 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” is false. Let's consider $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0+0 \\\\ 0+(-2) \\\\ 2+0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y+z & =2 \\\\ 2(0)-(-2)+(2) & =2 \\\\ 4 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is false. Since statements “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” and “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376068 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186669334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b6fdf40e-16e2-5d4d-87e5-0c2a5c46b067" :question/math-academy-id "q-157475" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $3x+2y+z=1$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ 1-3x-2y\\end{bmatrix},\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$Π$ contains the zero-vector” is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2y+z & =1 \\\\ 3(0)+2(0)+(0) & =1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” is false. Let's consider $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0+1 \\\\ 0+(-1) \\\\ 1+0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2y+z & =1 \\\\ 3(1)+2(-1)+(1) & =1 \\\\ 2 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is false. Since statements “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” and “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377575 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8426bbc0-cdc8-595a-941b-da128b255dca" :question/math-academy-id "q-36067" :question/problem "Consider the lines $3y-kx=3$ and $x+2y=0$, where $k$ is a constant. If both intersect at one point, then" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if the lines $-kx+3y=3$ and $x+2y=0$ intersect at one point, then\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-k & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis invertible and $\\det(A)\\ne 0$, which implies that ${A}^{-1}$ exists ($A$ is non-singular). Additionally, the unique solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is $\\mathbf{x}=⟨0,0⟩$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-k & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the lines."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377668 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aafb0544-5c12-5527-bc2a-2417215aad99" :question/math-academy-id "q-73566" :question/problem "Let \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det(A) & =\\det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. \n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement “$A$ is **not** invertible” is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “The lines $2x-3y=1$ and $2x-2y=2$ intersect at exactly one point” is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “$A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has no solution” is false since, according to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\nTherefore, only statement “The lines $2x-3y=1$ and $2x-2y=2$ intersect at exactly one point” is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377735 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07ba9ea7-a44e-52e4-bff2-32246bba4f40" :question/math-academy-id "q-78576" :question/problem "If $U=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${U}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${U}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{U}^{2} & =U\\cdot U \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${U}^{2}=U,$ so $U$ is an idempotent matrix.\n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${U}^{3}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{U}^{3} & ={U}^{2}\\cdot U \\\\  & =U\\cdot U \\\\  & ={U}^{2} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{U}^{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377756 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0131f756-450c-5ffd-bd43-d0c24b54342a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79352" :question/problem "Find $\\text{rank}(A)$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Null}(A)=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right.\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\text{nullity}(A)=3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+3 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377763 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c7ff497-8845-5d34-a5a1-e0b10d7a1e25" :question/math-academy-id "q-79367" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix with $3$ pivot columns?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is }\\text{invertible}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Col}(A) &=\\mathbb{R}^{5} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(A)) &=5=\\operatorname{rank}(A) \\\\ \\operatorname{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Null}(A)) &=0=\\operatorname{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(A)=3$ is true. Indeed, since $A$ has $3$ pivot columns, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is not  invertible” is true. Indeed, for the matrix $A,$ the number of the columns is $n=5.$ Now, since $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem,  $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(A)=3$ is false. According to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{rank}(A)=3$; “$A$ is not  invertible” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378566 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df2041e4-731a-5917-8788-670168bfca15" :question/math-academy-id "q-128453" :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=⟨8,4,6⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨8,-4,5⟩,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨0,2,k⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{vmatrix}-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{vmatrix}+6\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8(-4\\cdot k-2\\cdot 5)-4(8\\cdot k-0\\cdot 5)+6(8\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-4)) \\\\  & =-32k-80-32k+96 \\\\  & =16-64k \\\\  & =16(1-4k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =0 \\\\ 16(1-4k) & =0 \\\\ 1-4k & =0 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378573 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "57ae3b89-c4ab-5dbf-a8d7-b1b9339d2673" :question/math-academy-id "q-128482" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(3,0,1),$ $B(0,-1,3),$ $C(1,-2,2),$ and $D(2,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,-1,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3((-2)\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1)+((-2)\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 1)+2((-2)\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =6+1-12 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-5|=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378643 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fec19e3b-a382-541e-8dfb-bf5969fae1bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-130618" :question/problem "If $S=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 5 \\\\ -20 & -10\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${S}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "Computing ${S}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{2} & =S\\cdot S \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 5 \\\\ -20 & -10\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 & 5 \\\\ -20 & -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${S}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $S$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${S}^{3}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{3} & ={S}^{2}\\cdot S \\\\  & =O\\cdot S \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{S}^{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378650 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3acafe4-9ff1-5be0-a11c-248c3e1c6303" :question/math-academy-id "q-130622" :question/problem "If $R=\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 25 \\\\ -9 & 15\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${R}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "Computing ${R}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2} & =R\\cdot R \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 25 \\\\ -9 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 25 \\\\ -9 & 15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${R}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $R$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${R}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2} & ={R}^{2}\\cdot {R}^{2} \\\\  & =O\\cdot O \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{R}^{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378657 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbcc8a51-42ac-5cb8-a6bc-0647017d9ba5" :question/math-academy-id "q-130858" :question/problem "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $X$ and $Y$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(X)=14$, find $det(Y).$\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Y=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $Y$ can be obtained from $X$ by swapping the $2$nd and the $3$rd rows. \n\nThis changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =-det(X) \\\\  & =-(14) \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379355 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe6d904a-8846-5cf7-a75e-99f480622386" :question/math-academy-id "q-280772" :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{x}=⟨{k}^{2},-2,5⟩$, $\\mathbf{y}=⟨1,3,4⟩$, and $\\mathbf{z}=⟨0,-1,2⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n**In ascending order:** $\\,$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}\n$\\,$\nand\n$\\,$\n$k=${{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}\\cdot (\\mathbf{y}\\times \\mathbf{z}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{k}^{2} & -2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={k}^{2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={k}^{2}(3\\cdot 2-4\\cdot (-1))+2(1\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 0)+5(1\\cdot -1-3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & ={k}^{2}(6+4)+2(2)+5(-1) \\\\  & =10{k}^{2}+4-5 \\\\  & =10{k}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}\\cdot (\\mathbf{y}\\times \\mathbf{z}) & =0 \\\\ 10{k}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ 10{k}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ k & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if \n$k=-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{10}}$ or $k=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{10}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379372 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "44f0704d-d888-58ac-9a01-4ee289c5ce3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-32425" :question/problem "If $C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${C}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${C}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{2} & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${C}^{2}=C,$ so $C$ is an idempotent matrix.\n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${C}^{3}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{3} & ={C}^{2}\\cdot C \\\\  & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & ={C}^{2} \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379379 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94b8854f-eadf-5679-a6d5-099961aad0a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-32427" :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then ${B}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${B}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${B}^{2}=B,$ so $B$ is an idempotent matrix.\n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${B}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{4} & ={B}^{2}\\cdot {B}^{2} \\\\  & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & ={B}^{2} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{B}^{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379980 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "409f716a-c0ac-55a0-a174-94c6fcabcec4" :question/math-academy-id "q-72742" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $det(X)=10.$ What is the value of $det(6X)?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot X)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(X).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=6$ and $n=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(6X) & ={6}^{3}\\cdot det(X) \\\\  & =216\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =2160.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379987 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fd9cb32-6a50-5911-803f-5c6dda15fae8" :question/math-academy-id "q-72745" :question/problem "Suppose $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(C)=-2.$ What is the value of $det(-3C)?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot C)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=-3$ and $n=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(-3C) & =(-3)^{4}\\cdot det(C) \\\\  & =81\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-162.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379994 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9a9e509-4293-5590-b2bf-432eea2f6134" :question/math-academy-id "q-73749" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3y &=1 \\\\ 2x+6y &=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system." :question/worked-solution "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 3\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3y &=1 \\\\ 0x+0y &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0x+0y=1$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=1,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380090 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26013839-bad8-5e18-94c9-d8c0f29f855d" :question/math-academy-id "q-78573" :question/problem "If $V=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${V}^{7}=$" :question/worked-solution "Computing ${V}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{2} & =V\\cdot V \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${V}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $V$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${V}^{7}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{7} & ={V}^{2}\\cdot {V}^{5} \\\\  & =O\\cdot {V}^{5} \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{V}^{7}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380097 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7861f5f-5e43-5ff5-8123-034b760f1322" :question/math-academy-id "q-78574" :question/problem "If \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${P}^{6}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${P}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{2} & =P\\cdot P \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =P\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${P}^{2}=P,$ so $P$ is an idempotent matrix.\n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${P}^{6}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{6} & ={P}^{2}\\cdot {P}^{2}\\cdot {P}^{2} \\\\  & =P\\cdot P\\cdot P \\\\  & ={P}^{2}\\cdot P \\\\  & =P\\cdot P \\\\  & ={P}^{2} \\\\  & =P\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{P}^{6}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380915 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca4ecbae-990a-5f1f-aeca-c9cba7c7191b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131501" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "The system corresponding to $M$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3y-5z &=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove these equations from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\nx+3y-5z=1\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables $y$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y,z.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\nx=1-3y+5z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1-3y+5z \\\\ y &=y \\\\ z &=z,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$, $z$ (free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-3y+5z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+y\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+z\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381146 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1896da03-9822-50e1-8935-797efb12d1b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-157255" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0.$ is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =-3\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$” is false. Since statement $-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0.$ is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ for ${x}_{1}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=1.$\n\n- Statement “The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent” is true. Since statement $-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0.$ is true, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0.$; “The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382256 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "53b328c9-6698-53f6-9c7d-d6d44c5e77f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-73533" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & k & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $\\frac{1}{18}$ and $det(A)=-18.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=-18\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{31}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{31} & =(-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ k & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ k & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{13}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{31} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}\\cdot (2+k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{13}^{′}=\\frac{1}{18}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{18}(2+k) & =\\frac{1}{18} \\\\ 2+k & =-1 \\\\ k & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382854 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e00386b5-a859-5a68-9df5-ac8710a4ede8" :question/math-academy-id "q-106942" :question/problem "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:* \n\n*If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are vectors such that $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{x}$ for some $\\mathbf{x}\\in V,$ then $\\mathbf{u}=\\mathbf{v}.$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{x} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{given}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{x})+(-\\mathbf{x}) & =(\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{x})+(-\\mathbf{x}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{add }-\\mathbf{x}\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{u}+(\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})) & =\\mathbf{v}+(\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A2}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{0} & =\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\mathbf{v} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ll}{\\color{black}\\text{[A1]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A5]}}:{\\color{blue}1\\cdot \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A2]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})+\\mathbf{z}=\\mathbf{x}+(\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z})} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A6]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{x})=(\\alpha \\beta )\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A3]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{0}=\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A7]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\alpha +\\beta )\\mathbf{x}=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\beta \\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A4]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{0}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A8]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\alpha \\mathbf{y}}\\end{array}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{x} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{given}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{x})+(-\\mathbf{x}) & =(\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{x})+(-\\mathbf{x}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{add }-\\mathbf{x}\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{u}+(\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})) & =\\mathbf{v}+(\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A2}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{0} & =\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{axiom }{\\color{blue}\\text{A4}}\\hspace{0.167em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\mathbf{v} &  & [\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{axiom }{\\color{blue}\\text{A3}}\\hspace{0.167em}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383167 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fcc8498e-0bc7-505b-8119-49f146adfbcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-130729" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(X)=-60.$ The matrix $Y$ is obtained from $X$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{4}:=-\\frac{4}{15}{R}_{4}.$ What is the value of $det(Y)?$" :question/worked-solution "The row operation applied to $X$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $-\\frac{4}{15}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =-\\frac{4}{15}det(X) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{15}\\cdot (-60) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383174 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0fe57e28-2aac-5d10-8bff-5bf5dad424f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-130730" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $det(X)=-48.$ The matrix $Y$ is obtained from $X$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(Y)?$" :question/worked-solution "The row operation applied to $X$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =\\frac{1}{4}det(X) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-48) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383181 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ecc2dd8-459c-57bc-a2ce-f5ea52a64697" :question/math-academy-id "q-130731" :question/problem "Suppose $C$ is a $9\\times 9$ matrix and $det(C)=-93.$ The matrix $D$ is obtained from $C$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{9}:=-\\frac{5}{31}{R}_{9}.$ What is the value of $det(D)?$" :question/worked-solution "The row operation applied to $C$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $-\\frac{5}{31}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =-\\frac{5}{31}det(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{31}\\cdot (-93) \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383188 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ebaf64aa-970f-516d-b9fc-cd3f9155f1a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-130736" :question/problem "Suppose $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(C)=5.$ What is the value of $det(-3C)?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot C)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=-3$ and $n=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(3C) & =(-3)^{4}\\cdot det(C) \\\\  & =81\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =405.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383195 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a5f04aa-dd8a-5322-9e5d-a0849f397bd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-130737" :question/problem "Suppose $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(C)=-5.$ What is the value of $det(-2C)?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot C)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=-2$ and $n=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(-2C) & =(-2)^{4}\\cdot det(C) \\\\  & =16\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-80.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383202 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b572280-0021-5039-9903-b8cbf3a6e8d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-130742" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(A)=23.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}$ and ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+5{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+5{R}_{3}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we only have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(23) \\\\  & =-23.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383209 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c6c7b9b-1b7d-58b7-ad39-d5756aa91423" :question/math-academy-id "q-130743" :question/problem "Suppose $U$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(U)=21.$ The matrix $V$ is obtained from $U$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-7){R}_{4}$ and ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(V)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-7){R}_{4}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}$ involves multiplying a row by $\\frac{1}{3},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $\\frac{1}{3}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we only have to multiply the determinant by $\\frac{1}{3}.$ Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(V) & =\\frac{1}{3}det(U) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 21 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383216 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "38a459ba-c9b0-57c4-8ac3-81669bb1022e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130848" :question/problem "Given that $det(A)=det(B),$ which of the following elementary row operations transforms matrix $A$ to matrix $B,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -8 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $det(A)=det(B)$, the row operation applied to $A$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\n{R}_{j}:={R}_{j}+(k){R}_{i},\\,i\\ne j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{3} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -8 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383279 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc5890e5-c245-5798-b10d-ed7cf51b232f" :question/math-academy-id "q-131382" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${A}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}-6 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}0 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}}\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}-6 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}0 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 6\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${A}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383286 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0502d3a-1260-56b0-b010-809a7a1a12de" :question/math-academy-id "q-131980" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[A\\,|\\,I] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}-2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[A\\,|\\,I] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}-2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${A}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383633 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc6d9b64-fae9-5e89-abfa-9eb8e573a546" :question/math-academy-id "q-181683" :question/problem "![](images/e2/e2591e9da960008980cf96184975ef19713735814183a97ca8c8f784536fced5.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,-x-3\\,{{field-2}}\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,x+4\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-x-3$ and *below* the line $y=x+4.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-x-3,\\hspace{2em}y<x+4.\n$$\n\n \n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n-x-3<y<x+4.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the lines $y=-x-3$ and $y=x+4$ since the boundaries are included in the set (the lines are solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbols above as follows:\n\n$$\n-x-3\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}x+4\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-x-3\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}x+4\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383659 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6fe75e6b-cd24-5aa3-ab89-59af0e344d4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-182017" :question/problem "![](images/45/45690fd9ef862b1c8f0a2bb34bba2d305cd3c723ccde09648133b64006ad8f25.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,2\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in \\mathbb{Z},y\\in \\mathbb{Z},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{Z}\\times \\mathbb{Z}={\\mathbb{Z}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line $y=2.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<2\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is *not* included in the set (the curve is *not* solid),  no further action must be taken, so we have\n\n$$\ny\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{Z}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}2\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385324 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c7e8b0d2-1b58-5e69-8702-c53196883e47" :question/math-academy-id "q-35627" :question/problem "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Given any $\\mathbf{x}\\in V$, if $\\mathbf{w}$ is a vector such that $\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w}=\\mathbf{0}$, then $\\mathbf{w}=-\\mathbf{x}$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{given}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w})+(-\\mathbf{x}) & =\\mathbf{0}+(-\\mathbf{x}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{add }-\\mathbf{x}\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{x})+(-\\mathbf{x}) & =(-\\mathbf{x})+\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A1}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{x})+(-\\mathbf{x}) & =-\\mathbf{x} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A3}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+(\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})) & =-\\mathbf{x} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{0} & =-\\mathbf{x} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A4}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w} & =-\\mathbf{x} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ll}{\\color{black}\\text{[A1]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A5]}}:{\\color{blue}1\\cdot \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A2]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})+\\mathbf{z}=\\mathbf{x}+(\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z})} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A6]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{x})=(\\alpha \\beta )\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A3]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{0}=\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A7]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\alpha +\\beta )\\mathbf{x}=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\beta \\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A4]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{0}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A8]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\alpha \\mathbf{y}}\\end{array}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{given}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w})+(-\\mathbf{x}) & =\\mathbf{0}+(-\\mathbf{x}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{add }-\\mathbf{x}\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{x})+(-\\mathbf{x}) & =(-\\mathbf{x})+\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A1}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{x})+(-\\mathbf{x}) & =-\\mathbf{x} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A3}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+(\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})) & =-\\mathbf{x} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom }{\\color{blue}\\text{A2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{0} & =-\\mathbf{x} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A4}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w} & =-\\mathbf{x} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom }{\\color{blue}\\text{A3}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385422 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d2eb3a4-1529-5361-9598-a0c5afbbd3cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-42491" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=6.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ (switching the first and the second rows). What is the value of $det(B)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(6) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385429 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "08d3c76b-0a8e-5160-a10d-28283569af91" :question/math-academy-id "q-42492" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(A)=8.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{1}:=2{R}_{1}$ and ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:=2{R}_{1}$ involves multiplying a row by $2,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $2$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we only have to multiply the determinant by $2.$ Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =2det(A) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385442 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f2538da-8d51-59ef-9c88-dd06d7af1438" :question/math-academy-id "q-43047" :question/problem "Given that $det(A)=det(B),$ which of the following elementary row operations transforms matrix $A$ to matrix $B,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $det(A)=det(B)$, the row operation applied to $A$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\n{R}_{j}:={R}_{j}+(k){R}_{i},\\,i\\ne j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385557 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "457d76f4-06af-5356-9e00-91c260ff5a27" :question/math-academy-id "q-72725" :question/problem "Suppose $C$ is a $9\\times 9$ matrix and $det(C)=-69.$ The matrix $D$ is obtained from $C$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{5}↔{R}_{8}$ (switching the fifth and the eighth rows). What is the value of $det(D)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =-det(C) \\\\  & =-(-69) \\\\  & =69.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385564 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c250701-0a7a-5fa6-bb64-c33343cbfdd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-72749" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $det(X)=39.$ The matrix $Y$ is obtained from $X$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{4}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{4}$ and ${R}_{5}:={R}_{5}+(-3){R}_{1}.$ What is the value of $det(Y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{5}:={R}_{5}+(-3){R}_{1}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{4}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{4}$ involves multiplying a row by $\\frac{1}{3},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $\\frac{1}{3}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we only have to multiply the determinant by $\\frac{1}{3}.$ Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =\\frac{1}{3}det(X) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 39 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385571 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7dd7b936-dcd4-5588-8ef8-d567f8396fba" :question/math-academy-id "q-72751" :question/problem "Suppose $Y$ is a $9\\times 9$ matrix and $det(Y)=84.$ The matrix $Z$ is obtained from $Y$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{3}↔{R}_{7}$ and ${R}_{6}:={R}_{6}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}.$ What is the value of $det(Z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}↔{R}_{7}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{6}:={R}_{6}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we only have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Z) & =-det(Y) \\\\  & =-(84) \\\\  & =-84.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385599 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc5f69a3-44de-59cd-8de4-103061c156c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-75607" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $S$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${S}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[S\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}}\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $S$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $S$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[S\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${S}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{S}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385831 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96e3d27f-a6f3-58f8-a748-ec53b79d0713" :question/math-academy-id "q-94808" :question/problem "Let $Y$ be the set of all ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$  such that $x+y\\le 3.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$Y$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to the usual component-wise addition and scalar multiplication in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$Y$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to the usual component-wise addition and scalar multiplication in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$) because **multiplication of $(x,y)\\in Y$ by a scalar is not closed.***   \n\nFor example, $(1,1)\\in Y$ because $1+1=2\\le 3.$ However, if we add the components of $2\\cdot (1,1)=(2,2),$ we get $2+2=4>3.$ So, $2\\cdot (1,1)\\notin Y.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition in $Y$ is associative. $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- The zero element in $Y$ is $(0,0).$ ${\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication in $Y$ satisfy the distributive laws. $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- Addition in $Y$ is commutative. $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386048 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f36e193-cfb7-556d-862d-7ca0f147ebca" :question/math-academy-id "q-102799" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3y &=-x \\\\ 2y &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3y &=0 \\\\ 2y &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386055 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "852dcdfe-ad8b-55fc-ab02-b3cb773c55c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-103533" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\ngiven that $det(A)=4.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386120 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3e014c9-7e0f-5613-9d77-d53b9ab4992b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105102" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{w}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $4\\mathbf{v}+2\\mathbf{w}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply each component of $\\mathbf{v}$ by $4$ and each component of $\\mathbf{w}$ by $2.$ Then, we add the vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\mathbf{v}+2\\mathbf{w} & =4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 18 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}20+(-2) \\\\ -12+18 \\\\ 8+(-4) \\\\ -4+6 \\\\ 0+(-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386134 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ebd8d026-a8f0-5c49-8cd6-0d2ba4d731b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-105157" :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{x}$, if $2\\mathbf{a}+4\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $\\mathbf{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\mathbf{a}+4\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ 4\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{b}-2\\mathbf{a} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-2\\mathbf{a}) \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386585 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "500f0d4b-f4d9-5169-9a06-c653e34353e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-110855" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},$ ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -16\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 3 & -16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 3 & -16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-8){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 11 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386592 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ee4526d-564d-5328-b11a-a4978f3ab8b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-110909" :question/problem "The vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1},$ ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ are given below. Determine whether $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ where ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}{x}_{1}}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}}+{\\color{rblack}{x}_{2}}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2} &=-6 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}+18{x}_{2} &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 18 & 12\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 18 & 12\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 24\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second row of the RREF matrix above, we see that the system of equations has no solution.\n\nTherefore, $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386599 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "413cbee8-b598-5cbf-b19b-566a259d8943" :question/math-academy-id "q-112242" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2} & =\\mathbf{x} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -3 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -3 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -9\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -9\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -15\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=3 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=5.$ \n\nTherefore, $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=3+5=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387318 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33942b97-95c2-5a05-bee1-52e88505e10f" :question/math-academy-id "q-12284" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-2y &=3 \\\\ 2x-y &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387852 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78e0dc1f-5e12-54f0-b00c-492a979933d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-127937" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,-1,3)$ and is perpendicular to the line that goes through the points \n$A(-4,1,3)$ and $B(2,3,5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨2,-1,3⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(-4,1,3)$ and $B(2,3,5)$ we can find a vector parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨2,3,5⟩-⟨-4,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,2,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨6,2,2⟩.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩ & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩ & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387873 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a275fd86-a21a-5ef1-ae5c-9df806e069b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-128400" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $7x+y-3=0$ of the plane $\\mathrm{Π}$, find an equation of $\\mathrm{Π}$ in the form $(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n}=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $\\mathrm{Π}$ is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨7,1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\mathrm{Π}$ for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x+y-3 & =0 \\\\ y & =3-7x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$, we get $y=3.$ Hence, the point $P(0,3,0)$ lies on $\\mathrm{Π}.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨0,3,0⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ (\\mathbf{r}-⟨0,3,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨7,1,0⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387887 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d8e167e-ba7b-5746-8896-10f0f88087c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-128501" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $A(7,-2,3)$ and is parallel to the lines $\\mathbf{r}=⟨9,2,1⟩+s⟨2,-2,1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨4,-5,19⟩+t⟨3,4,7⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The direction vectors of the lines are ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=⟨2,-2,1⟩$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=⟨3,4,7⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $A(7,-2,3)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $\\mathbf{a}=⟨7,-2,3⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{a}+s{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+t{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨7,-2,3⟩+s⟨2,-2,1⟩+t⟨3,4,7⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387957 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d8ae8a52-91d4-5f03-bbaf-4e5a85108c69" :question/math-academy-id "q-128741" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $P(1,-2,3),\\hspace{0.167em}Q(3,-4,2),$ and $R(4,3,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨3,-4,2⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =⟨4,3,2⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,5,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $\\mathbf{p}=⟨1,-2,3⟩$ of the point $P,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{p}+s\\overset{\\to }{PQ}+t\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨1,-2,3⟩+s⟨2,-2,-1⟩+t⟨3,5,-1⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387964 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0a7f0779-a02e-54b4-a490-a401988e7022" :question/math-academy-id "q-128745" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $D(3,2,-1),\\hspace{0.167em}E(3,1,-2),$ and $F(5,1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =⟨3,1,-2⟩-⟨3,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =⟨5,1,2⟩-⟨3,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $\\mathbf{d}=⟨3,2,-1⟩$ of the point $D,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{d}+s\\overset{\\to }{DE}+t\\overset{\\to }{DF} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨3,2,-1⟩+s⟨0,-1,-1⟩+t⟨2,-1,3⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388118 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d89d4cfc-04ae-58b4-94a3-0a7fbe7e0bc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-130591" :question/problem "If $B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${B}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "Computing ${B}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${B}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $B$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${B}^{3}$ quickly, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{3} & ={B}^{2}\\cdot B \\\\  & =O\\cdot B \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  \n\n$$\n{B}^{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388125 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce36ac4d-8289-51ae-b2d5-2c062b43f9e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-130606" :question/problem "If $C=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ -9 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${C}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "Computing ${C}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{2} & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ -9 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ -9 & -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${C}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $C$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${C}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{2} & ={C}^{2}\\cdot {C}^{2} \\\\  & =O\\cdot O \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{C}^{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388174 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "27064447-2648-5562-9eb6-77f50dae0ce5" :question/math-academy-id "q-130822" :question/problem "What is the minor of ${q}_{22}$ in \n$$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 8 & \\sqrt{3} & -1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 19 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 8 \\\\ -7 & 4 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get the minor of ${q}_{22}$, we remove the $2$nd row and $2$nd column:\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {\\color{gray}∗} & \\sqrt{3} & -1 \\\\ {\\color{gray}∗} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} & {\\color{gray}∗} & {\\color{gray}∗} \\\\ 0 & {\\color{gray}∗} & 0 & 8 \\\\ -7 & {\\color{gray}∗} & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor is \n\n$$\n{M}_{22}=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{3} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 \\\\ -7 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388188 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ead9650-2d18-5f36-b35f-cc2f4e8392b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-130905" :question/problem "Find the cofactor of ${g}_{42}$ in \n$$\n\\displaystyle G=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 & 2 & \\sqrt{3} & -2 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & \\sqrt{7} & 1 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -5 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 4 & \\sqrt{5} & 3 & 8 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 3 & 0 & -9 & 11\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, to get the minor of ${g}_{42}$, we remove the $4$th row and $2$nd column: \n\n$$\nG=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & {\\color{gray}∗} & 2 & \\sqrt{3} & -2 \\\\ 3 & {\\color{gray}∗} & 0 & \\sqrt{7} & 1 \\\\ 0 & {\\color{gray}∗} & -5 & 6 & -4 \\\\ {\\color{gray}∗} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{gray}\\sqrt{5}}} & {\\color{gray}∗} & {\\color{gray}∗} & {\\color{gray}∗} \\\\ 11 & {\\color{gray}∗} & 0 & -9 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor is \n\n$$\n{M}_{42}=\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 2 & \\sqrt{3} & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & \\sqrt{7} & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 11 & 0 & -9 & 11\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the cofactor of ${g}_{42}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{42} & =(-1)^{4+2}{M}_{42} \\\\  & ={M}_{42} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 2 & \\sqrt{3} & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & \\sqrt{7} & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 11 & 0 & -9 & 11\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388209 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23d98e62-6043-5354-9621-545d60a12bf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-131379" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${A}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}}\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${A}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388251 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16ee071c-dc61-52a6-97dd-99854448ee3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-132003" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 4 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -5 & -4 & 8 \\\\ {\\color{red}{-1}} & {\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{2}} & {\\color{red}{-2}} \\\\ -11 & 7 & 9 & -6\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{32}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{32}=1$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 4 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -5 & -4 & 8 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -11 & 7 & 9 & -6\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-2){C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+2{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 & -6 & 15 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 7 & -5 & 8\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 & -6 & 15 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 7 & -5 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -6 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -5 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -6 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -5 & 8\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388862 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a3cbd65-d830-59dc-8511-5f72908033d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-142485" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $4\\times 5$ matrix. If $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$, find $\\text{dim(Null}(A)).$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=5,$ and since $B$ is formed by $2$ vectors, $\\text{rank}(A)=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 2+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-2 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390360 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "765f0999-da93-5e44-b207-672576084830" :question/math-academy-id "q-19779" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,0,3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨2,5,3⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 5)\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0-15)\\mathbf{i}-(3-6)\\mathbf{j}+(5-0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-15,3,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390367 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a05ecba-dbf9-513d-af2d-03ee8f54c56e" :question/math-academy-id "q-20508" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -3\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390374 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b0ab2ea-5bc8-5361-b6e4-ca31cdf1350a" :question/math-academy-id "q-20539" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 9 & -13 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 17 & -26\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 9 & -13 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 17 & -26\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 17 & -26\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390388 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0787607-5bca-5e97-b7f4-40db59c2425f" :question/math-academy-id "q-20963" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 8 & 16 & -8\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 8 & 16 & -8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-8){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=-1 \\\\ 0x+0y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx+2y=-1.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=-1-2y,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-1-2y \\\\ y &=y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1-2y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390395 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1766ca49-3fde-5817-b4dd-1d97ee2424fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-20965" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+15y &=10 \\\\ 2x+6y &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system." :question/worked-solution "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}5 & 15 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 4\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}5 & 15 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3y &=2 \\\\ 0x+0y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx+3y=2.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=2-3y,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2-3y \\\\ y &=y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2-3y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390694 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c60618eb-8102-5d29-9fef-37f3fce33d0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-32026" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-2y &=1 \\\\ 8x+3y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot 8}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{31}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{31}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390711 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ffd8425a-ff52-5722-b741-7d19394751e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-32136" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y &=-1 \\\\ 2x+3y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390718 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34c426c5-7657-5020-93fd-26e20c441212" :question/math-academy-id "q-32139" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+6y &=46 \\\\ 8x+y &=-23\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}46 \\\\ -23\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1-6\\cdot 8}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{46}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{46}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}46 \\\\ -23\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390750 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7c08cd8-33d8-5be3-943c-1859738a0181" :question/math-academy-id "q-32436" :question/problem "If \n$D=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${D}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${D}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{2} & =D\\cdot D \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${D}^{2}=D,$ so $D$ is an idempotent matrix.\n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${D}^{3}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{3} & ={D}^{2}\\cdot D \\\\  & =D\\cdot D \\\\  & ={D}^{2} \\\\  & =D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{D}^{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390791 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c52202b7-1fa1-5c69-a358-e874710d6248" :question/math-academy-id "q-32798" :question/problem "What is the minor of ${f}_{13}$ in  $$\n\\displaystyle F=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 8 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 11 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get the minor of ${f}_{13}$, we remove the $1$st row and $3$rd column:\n\n$$\nF=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{gray}∗} & {\\color{gray}∗} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{5} & {\\color{gray}∗} & {\\color{gray}∗} \\\\ 0 & 7 & {\\color{gray}∗} & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & {\\color{gray}∗} & 4 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5 & {\\color{gray}∗} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & {\\color{gray}∗} & 11 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n   \n\nSo, the minor is\n\n$$\n{M}_{13}=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 7 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 11 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390848 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8d650e6-dfa8-594c-8c20-f63e265d1d5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-33026" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point  $Q(3,2,0)$ and is  perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}=⟨1,-2,5⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $Q$ is $\\mathbf{q}=⟨3,2,0⟩,$ and $\\mathbf{n}=⟨1,-2,5⟩$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $Q({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{q})\\cdot \\mathbf{n}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $\\mathbf{q}$ is the position vector of $Q.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{q})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{q}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{q}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨1,-2,5⟩ & =⟨3,2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-2,5⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨1,-2,5⟩ & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391180 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aa8c997f-15c5-5b2a-b1e2-bd26aa6233d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-33324" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,0,4)$ and is  perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2-t \\\\ y &=4+3t \\\\ z &=-6+5t.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨-3,0,4⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2-t \\\\ y &=4+3t \\\\ z &=-6+5t\\end{aligned}\\right.\\hspace{1em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨2,4,-6⟩+t\\text{ }\\underbrace{⟨-1,3,5⟩}_{\\mathbf{v}}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $\\mathbf{v}=⟨-1,3,5⟩.$ So, a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\mathbf{v}=⟨-1,3,5⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-1,3,5⟩ & =⟨-3,0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,3,5⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-1,3,5⟩ & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391682 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b07ac22-3309-5bc0-ac4b-6e1be0e5a45e" :question/math-academy-id "q-33823" :question/problem "Find a normal vector to the plane\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=⟨1,1,0⟩+t⟨-1,1,1⟩+s⟨0,2,2⟩,\\hspace{1em}t,\\hspace{0.167em}s\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the parametric equation, we have that $\\mathbf{v}=⟨-1,1,1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{w}=⟨0,2,2⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{v}\\times \\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{array}} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨0,2,-2⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391769 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df695587-ec35-5395-87f6-af8d0149e4cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-34158" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $\\mathbf{c}+\\mathbf{d}.$" :question/worked-solution "We add the vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}+\\mathbf{d} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2-3 \\\\ 0+2 \\\\ -1+5 \\\\ 4+7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391776 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8972b624-d396-5686-85e2-b86ff24317e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-34164" :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$, then $-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}=$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying each component of $\\mathbf{b}$ by $-\\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b} & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391783 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "815b0df7-6a48-559f-aa63-735c856d741c" :question/math-academy-id "q-34228" :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}$, then $-\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{c}=$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying each component of $\\mathbf{c}$ by $-\\frac{1}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{c} & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 12 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-8) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 16\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
